INT. SAFEKEEP SELF-STORAGE - UNIT 114 - DAY

ON CAMERA. A handheld camcorder, the red light blinking. Orange roll-up door half raised, daylight in a bar across a concrete floor. Dust in the air. Boxes. A dressmaker's mannequin in a 1960s wedding gown yellowed to the colour of tea. A Singer sewing machine under a quilt. A record player. Stacks of Life magazines.

LORNA PRESCOTT, 60s, cardigan, reading glasses on a chain, stands beside WALT PRESCOTT, 70, her brother, in a windbreaker and a cap that says Korea Veteran, and behind them, filming, INES, 20s, their niece, her sleeves rolled, the camera at her chest.

Silence.

Dust.

LORNA
(running a finger along the mannequin's sleeve)
She'd have hated this lighting.

WALT
(not looking up from a box)
Everybody hates that lighting. Doesn't mean it's wrong.

INES
(from behind the camera, gently)
Aunt Lorna, can you say what this is for the tape?

LORNA
(sighing, turning to face the lens)
This is Mother's wedding dress. Nineteen sixty-two. She made it herself on that machine.

Nothing.

A truck outside. Reversing.

WALT
(lifting out a record, reading the sleeve)
Patsy Cline. I told you she kept the records.

LORNA
(not unkindly)
You told me she kept nothing.

WALT
(setting it down very carefully)
I was wrong about a lot of things in 1985.

Silence.

Ines pans, slowly. A shelf of mason jars. Buttons. A tin of thread. A child's shoe.

INES
(softly)
Whose is the shoe?

LORNA
(after a long moment, picking it up)
Mine. I think. Or Walter's. It's too small for either of us now.

WALT
(taking it from her hands, turning it over)
Mine. See the heel. I wore it down running from Dad.

LORNA
(laughing despite herself)
You never ran from Dad.

WALT
(smiling, not looking at her)
Oh, I ran.

Dust. Light.

INES
(lowering the camera slightly, then raising it again)
Should we keep any of it?

LORNA
(placing the shoe in her handbag, closing the clasp)
That. And the dress.

WALT
(nodding, tapping the Singer)
And this.

LORNA
(wryly)
You don't sew.

WALT
(grinning)
I might start.

Quiet. The three of them stand in the bar of daylight, looking at the unit, the long life of one woman in a ten-by-ten room.

INES
(whispering)
Okay. That's the whole unit. I'm turning it off.

LORNA
(reaching toward the lens)
Leave it on a minute more.

The red light blinks. Nothing happens. Nothing happens for a very long time.
